    "searchText": "Question: Subsequent events and impact on audit report \nYou are the audit manager on the audit of the financial statements of Fairview Bank Ltd \n(‘Fairview’) for the year ended 31 March 2X19. Following completion of the year-end audit \nfieldwork, you are reviewing the list of subsequent events prepared by Fairview’s finance \ndirector.  \nFairview is a small independent retail bank and investment house, whose customers comprise \nsmall to medium size companies and high-earning professional individuals. The bank operates \nfrom a branch in Birmingham, but attracts worldwide clients. It has full banking licences and \nhas been trading successfully for five years.  \nYour audit partner has reviewed the list of subsequent events and has added additional \ncomments as necessary (Appendix 1). She has asked you evaluate whether or not these are \nadjusting events and to consider what additional audit evidence, if any, would be required for \neach of these matters. She has also asked you to analyse the impact on the audit report, should \nthe additional audit evidence not be obtained and to recommend the appropriate type of audit \nopinion to be issued. \nQuestions \n1. Evaluate the subsequent event, concluding whether or not it would be an adjusting event  \n(12 marks) \n \n2. Consider what, if any, additional audit evidence would be required  \n(14 marks) \n \n3. (i) analyse the impact on the audit report if the issue were not satisfactorily resolved; and  \n(ii) recommend the appropriate type of audit opinion to be issued.  \n(12 marks) \n \n \n Appendix – List of subsequent events provided by finance director of Fairview \nNote: Materiality has been set at £2.2 million and trivial threshold at £110,000 for audit \npurposes \n1. Post year-end takeover \nOn 10 June 2X19, the board announced that it had completed a takeover of Hi-Bank \nInvestments (‘Hi-Bank’), a credit union based in the Scottish Highlands. The board first \ndiscussed the takeover in May and takeover talks were completed on 10 June 2X19. \nAudit partner comments: \nThere was no previous indication of this potential deal during the year-end audit. We have \nreviewed the recent year-end financial statements for Hi-Bank. The post takeover assets of \nFairview will increase by 70%. \n2. Staff bonus accrual \nThe year-end staff bonus accrual of £1 million included in the year-end financial statements \nwas calculated based on the budgeted results for the financial year-end. Actual bonuses of \n£1.25 million have now been agreed by the board and paid to staff. Following the successful \nperformance of the bank, the bonuses paid were greater than those accrued.  \nThe board feels there will be little benefit in reflecting the actual paid figure in the year-end \nfinancial statements and would like to maintain the accrued balance at 31 March 2X19 which \nwas based on budgeted results. \nAudit partner comments: \nWe must assess the value of the bonuses paid and assess the level compared to the accrual \nmade. \n3. Financial Conduct Authority investigation \nThe Financial Conduct Authority (‘FCA’) commenced an investigation of Fairview in October \n2X17. The investigation stemmed from complaints to the regulatory authorities of ‘overly robust \nselling techniques’ for loans to a specialised area of the bank’s customers.  \nFor the financial year-end 31 March 2X18, a preliminary accrual was made of £2.75 million \nwhich represented £1.8 million for fines and £0.95 million for anticipated compensation costs.  \nWith the full cooperation of Fairview staff, the investigation was recently concluded and the FCA \nissued a report of their findings on 1 June 2019. Fairview accept the allegations and will be fined \nby the FCA and will have to pay compensation to each customer affected. The FCA report \nestimates the total costs that Fairview will have to pay will be in the region of £5 million. \nAudit partner comments: \nFrom discussions with the board, it is keen not to increase this provision. With the conclusion of \nthe FCA investigation and report now published it is time to finalise figures. Whilst the amounts \nbeing considered are considerable, it will not be significant enough to affect the going concern \nof the business. \n \n 4. Environmental, Social and Governance (‘ESG’) – ethical banking \nFairview has recognised that its current and potential customers are concerned with the bank’s \nethical policy on trading and its contribution to the wider community. Post year-end, the board \nof directors (‘the board’) engaged an external consultant to examine Fairview’s current ESG \npractices and suggest changes that it should implement. This report was not available during \nthe year-end audit but became available this week. \nFairview have added these suggestions to their sustainability report within the annual report. \nAudit partner comments: \nWe must consider the consistency of information presented in the financial statements and \nother sections of the annual report. \n",
